<mxfile host="Electron" agent="FirmFlake docs generator" pages="4">
  <diagram id="overview" name="1. Overview">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1426" dy="883" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1100" pageHeight="850" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <mxCell id="overview-title" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=4;" value="&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:22px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FirmFlake: how the code fits together&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font color=&quot;#666666&quot;&gt;Hover any block for a longer explanation. Blocks marked ↗ open a more detailed page (click the link icon, or Ctrl+click).&lt;/font&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="60" width="900" x="40" y="20" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="band1" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;opacity=35;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=88;spacingTop=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="① Projects" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="200" width="1080" x="20" y="100" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="band1s" parent="1" style="text;html=1;align=right;verticalAlign=top;fontColor=#555555;fontSize=12;fontStyle=2;" value="Where the main functions resides. (Program entry points)" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="22" width="490" x="600" y="103" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="band2" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;opacity=35;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=88;spacingTop=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="② common_libraries/" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="170" width="1080" x="20" y="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="band2s" parent="1" style="text;html=1;align=right;verticalAlign=top;fontColor=#555555;fontSize=12;fontStyle=2;" value="hardware-agnostic: works on any chip, or on a PC" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="22" width="490" x="600" y="323" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="band3" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffe6cc;strokeColor=#d79b00;opacity=35;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=88;spacingTop=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" value="③ platform/stm32g4xx/" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="170" width="1080" x="20" y="510" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="band3s" parent="1" style="text;html=1;align=right;verticalAlign=top;fontColor=#555555;fontSize=12;fontStyle=2;" value="hardware-specific: only works on STM32G4 chips" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="22" width="490" x="600" y="513" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;STM32 board project&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;i&gt;e.g. mini_rover_stm32g431cbu6/, PDB_24/&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;One folder per circuit board.&lt;br&gt;Holds the app code, pin setup, and build files." tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;STM32 board project (one folder per PCB)&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Contains only what is unique to that board:&lt;br&gt;• Core/Src/app/app_main.cpp: the application (what this board does)&lt;br&gt;• CubeMX generated files (main.c, gpio.c, fdcan.c ...): chip setup produced by STM32CubeMX from the .ioc file&lt;br&gt;• platform_defs.h / pinout_defs: which pin is wired to what on this PCB&lt;br&gt;• startup .s file + linker script .ld: how the program is laid out in the chip&#39;s flash/RAM&lt;br&gt;• Makefile + CMakeLists.txt: how to build and flash it&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Everything reusable is pulled in from common_libraries/ and platform/ instead of being copied.&lt;/div&gt;" id="stm32">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;align=left;spacingRight=240;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="150" width="500" x="110" y="135" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;Makefile&lt;/b&gt; ↗&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;Shortcuts: &lt;i&gt;make build&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;make flash&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/font&gt;" tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;Makefile: a list of shortcut commands.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Here it is only a thin wrapper: `make build` runs the longer cmake commands for you, `make flash` builds then writes the program onto the board with st-flash.&lt;br&gt;The real build logic is in CMakeLists.txt.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;(Open the link for the full build flow.)&lt;/div&gt;" link="data:page/id,build" id="make">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;spacingTop=2;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="50" width="210" x="380" y="147" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;CMakeLists.txt&lt;/b&gt; ↗&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;Picks which files get compiled&lt;/font&gt;" tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;CMakeLists.txt: the build recipe.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Tells CMake which .c/.cpp files to compile (target_sources), which folders to search for #include headers (target_include_directories), and which compiler to use (the toolchain file).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This is where a board &#39;chooses&#39; its pieces: it lists app code from its own folder, generic code from common_libraries/, and the STM32 implementations from platform/.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;(Open the link for the full build flow.)&lt;/div&gt;" link="data:page/id,build" id="cmake">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;spacingTop=2;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="50" width="210" x="380" y="223" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="e-make-cmake" edge="1" parent="1" source="make" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=1;html=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;strokeWidth=1.5;fontSize=11;labelBackgroundColor=#ffffff;" target="cmake" value="runs">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;RoverFlake2 repo&lt;/b&gt; &lt;font color=&quot;#666666&quot;&gt;(separate repo, FirmFlake is a git submodule in it)&lt;/font&gt;" tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;RoverFlake2 is the main rover software repo (ROS2, runs on the rover&#39;s onboard computer).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;It includes FirmFlake as a git submodule at RoverFlake2/FirmFlake, so its packages can reach into FirmFlake/common_libraries/ with a relative path.&lt;/div&gt;" id="rf2">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#9673a6;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;dashed=1;align=left;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="150" width="420" x="650" y="135" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;ROS2 node&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;i&gt;e.g. arm_hardware_interface, rover_hmi&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Runs on the rover&#39;s Linux computer, not a microcontroller." tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;ROS2 node (C++ on Linux)&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Talks to the microcontroller boards over serial/CAN. To agree on the message format, it compiles the SAME comms code the boards use (common_libraries/comms), pointing its own CMakeLists.txt at ../../FirmFlake/common_libraries/comms.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;It can do that only because common_libraries is hardware-agnostic. It never uses platform/ (that code needs an STM32).&lt;/div&gt;" id="ros">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#e1d5e7;strokeColor=#9673a6;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;align=left;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="90" width="380" x="670" y="180" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;Interfaces&lt;/b&gt; ↗&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;&quot;&lt;b&gt;What&lt;/b&gt; a part can do&quot;, no hardware code&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;i&gt;clock_interface, can_interface, primitive_io_base, servo_interface, sensor_interface&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/font&gt;" tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;Interface: a promise of what something can do, with no code that does it.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;e.g. ClockInterface says &#39;a clock has get_ms() and blocking_delay()&#39;, but says nothing about HOW (no STM32, no HAL).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;In C++ these are classes whose functions are all &#39;pure virtual&#39; (= 0).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;(Open the link for the clock_interface vs clock_stm32g4xx example.)&lt;/div&gt;" link="data:page/id,iface" id="iface">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;align=left;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="115" width="270" x="110" y="355" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;Generic libraries&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;Logic that works anywhere&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;i&gt;timer, stopwatch, comms, crc, low_pass_fir_filter, usr_led&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/font&gt;" tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;Generic (hardware-agnostic) libraries: real working code, but no chip-specific calls.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;When they need hardware, they ask for an interface instead. e.g. Timer takes a ClockInterface&amp;amp;, so the same timer.cpp works on an STM32, an ESP32, or in a PC unit test.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;comms/ and crc/ need no hardware at all, which is why RoverFlake2 can reuse them.&lt;/div&gt;" id="generic">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;align=left;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="115" width="290" x="430" y="355" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;Unit tests (Catch2)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;Run on your PC and in GitHub CI on every push&lt;br&gt;&lt;i&gt;own Makefile + CMakeLists&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/font&gt;" tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;common_libraries/ has its own Makefile + CMakeLists.txt, but they do NOT build firmware.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;They compile the libraries with your normal PC compiler (g++) together with the */test/test_*.cpp files, and run them with Catch2.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;`cd common_libraries &amp;amp;&amp;amp; make test`&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;GitHub Actions (.github/workflows/build_and_test_common_libs.yaml) runs the same command on every push.&lt;/div&gt;" id="tests">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#999999;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;align=left;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="115" width="290" x="780" y="355" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="e-tests" edge="1" parent="1" source="tests" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=1;html=1;dashed=1;endArrow=open;endFill=0;strokeWidth=1.5;fontSize=11;labelBackgroundColor=#ffffff;" target="generic" value="tests">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;Implementations&lt;/b&gt; ↗ &lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;(Modules/)&lt;/font&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;&quot;&lt;b&gt;How&lt;/b&gt; to do it on an STM32G4&quot;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;i&gt;clock_stm32g4xx, can_stm32g4xx, primitive_io_stm32g4xx, servo_stm32g4xx&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/font&gt;" tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;Implementation: the code that keeps an interface&#39;s promise on real hardware.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;e.g. ClockSTM32G4XX inherits from ClockInterface and fills in get_ms() by calling ST&#39;s HAL_GetTick().&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Naming: &amp;lt;thing&amp;gt;_&amp;lt;platform&amp;gt;. A future chip would get platform/&amp;lt;chip&amp;gt;/Modules/... with the same interfaces.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;(Open the link for the full example.)&lt;/div&gt;" link="data:page/id,iface" id="impl">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#ffe6cc;strokeColor=#d79b00;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;align=left;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="115" width="270" x="110" y="545" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="&lt;b&gt;HAL_Drivers&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;ST&#39;s own driver code (HAL + CMSIS).&lt;br&gt;One shared copy for every G4 board.&lt;/font&gt;" tooltip="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px;line-height:1.45;max-width:520px;padding:4px&quot;&gt;HAL = Hardware Abstraction Layer, written by ST (the chip maker).&lt;br&gt;CMSIS = ARM&#39;s standard headers for Cortex-M cores.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Functions like HAL_GetTick(), HAL_GPIO_WritePin(), HAL_FDCAN_AddMessageToTxFifoQ().&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Kept once in platform/stm32g4xx/HAL_Drivers/ instead of a copy inside every board project. Each board&#39;s cmake/stm32cubemx/CMakeLists.txt points here.&lt;/div&gt;" id="hal">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#ffe6cc;strokeColor=#d79b00;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;align=left;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="115" width="290" x="430" y="545" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="rule" parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;arcSize=6;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;verticalAlign=top;spacingTop=6;spacing=8;align=left;" value="&lt;b&gt;Rule of thumb&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;Touches HAL / registers / a specific chip? → &lt;b&gt;platform/&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Otherwise → &lt;b&gt;common_libraries/&lt;/b&gt; (and give it a unit test)&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Platform layer should be as &quot;thin&quot; as possible. This lets us switch hardware with minimal code changes.&lt;/font&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="115" width="290" x="780" y="545" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="e-stm32-generic" edge="1" parent="1" source="stm32" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=1;html=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;strokeWidth=1.5;fontSize=11;labelBackgroundColor=#ffffff;exitX=0.82;exitY=1;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0.31;entryY=0;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" target="generic" value="uses">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="e-stm32-impl" edge="1" parent="1" source="stm32" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=1;html=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;strokeWidth=1.5;fontSize=11;labelBackgroundColor=#ffffff;exitX=0;exitY=0.5;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" target="impl" value="uses">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
